**What was built** (all in `engine/platform`, pure and platform-neutral)
- **Action maps**: named button and axis actions. Bindings read keys, mouse buttons, mouse motion and wheel, gamepad buttons, and gamepad axes, with a slot or any pad. Each binding has a scale (`-1` turns a key pair into an axis) and a dead zone for gamepad axes. Several bindings on one action combine in the same frame.
- **Remapping**: per-action user overrides via `rebind`, `reset`, and `reset_all`. Invalid bindings are rejected and change nothing.
- **Saved profile**: JSON through the vendored nlohmann/json. It stores only overrides, carries a format name and version 1, and rejects other formats, versions, and unknown names. Apply is all-or-nothing, and save is atomic.
- **Capture routing**: `Editor` and `Ui` owners request capture per device class (keyboard, mouse, gamepad). Captured events never reach gameplay. Gameplay's held state is kept consistent with the physical devices, so a key held when capture starts is released for gameplay, and one held when capture ends is pressed.
- **Play**: entering Play releases all capture and refuses new requests until Edit.

**Interpretations I chose** (say if you want any changed)
- "Editor/UI capture" means per-device-class capture by owner, not per-widget hit testing. There is no UI yet to decide that.
- Entering Play refuses capture requests, so the editor cannot take input during Play.
- Button actions are down at a combined value of 0.5 or more. Axis actions clamp to [-1, 1].
- The scenario tests are labelled `integration`. Everything else is `unit`.

**Tests**: 4 new files, 42 cases total. Unit tests cover the action model, profiles, and the router. Scenario tests run keyboard, pad, and mouse together, remap and load a profile mid-session, and capture then Play.

